<section class="page-head">
  <p class="kicker">A sky note &middot; observation, interpretation, and the first black holes</p>
  <h1>JWST&rsquo;s Little Red Dots</h1>
  <p class="lede muted">They look almost trivial: compact red points in Webb&rsquo;s deep fields. Their spectra
    are not trivial at all. Many now appear to be young, rapidly growing black holes wrapped in dense gas
    &mdash; a brief phase that can imitate a galaxy, conceal an active nucleus, and change how astronomers
    reconstruct the first billion years.</p>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame frame--first illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">The answer as of 11 October 2026</p>
    <h2 id="t-short-answer">Small on the Detector, Large in the Story</h2>
    <p class="lede"><strong>The leading explanation is no longer simply &ldquo;an impossibly mature little
      galaxy&rdquo;.</strong> For the best-observed examples, the central engine is an accreting massive black
      hole enclosed by a compact, optically thick cocoon of ionised or partly ionised gas. The cocoon
      reprocesses the central light, suppresses familiar X-ray and radio signatures, and can broaden hydrogen
      lines by electron scattering. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
    <p>That conclusion is strong for several individual objects and for the broad-line subset. It is not a
      licence to declare every colour-selected dot identical. Some little red dots have narrow lines; faint
      stellar hosts are now detected statistically and around individual systems; and the balance among black
      hole light, gas emission, dust and stars differs from source to source. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
    <p>The useful sentence is therefore precise: <strong>little red dots are an observational class, not yet
      one guaranteed species.</strong> Many seem to mark an enshrouded phase of early black-hole growth.
      &ldquo;Black hole star&rdquo; is a vivid name for the gas-enshrouded configuration, not a claim that the event
      horizon performs nuclear fusion like an ordinary star.</p>
    <p class="muted small">Grading on this page: <span class="sci">&#9670; observation, established physics,
      or a result supported by direct analysis</span> &middot; <span class="hist">&#9670; publication history or
      scientific context</span> &middot; <span class="spec">&#9670; model-dependent inference or a testable proposal</span>
      &middot; <span class="myth">&#9670; philosophical analogy, explicitly separated from astronomy</span>.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider">✦</div>

<section class="wrap">
  <div class="page-head tight">
    <p class="kicker">First restraint</p>
    <h2 id="t-red">A Red Dot Is Not a Diagnosis</h2>
    <p class="lede muted">The name records an appearance in selected Webb images. It does not identify the
      engine that made the light.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="grid two">
    <div class="frame card illuminated">
      <h3>Cosmological redshift</h3>
      <p>Expansion stretches every emitted wavelength. If light left a source when the cosmic scale factor
        was \(a_{\rm em}\), then</p>
      <p class="formula">\[
        1+z=\frac{\lambda_{\rm obs}}{\lambda_{\rm em}}
        =\frac{a_0}{a_{\rm em}}.
      \]</p>
      <p>At \(z=6\), a rest-frame wavelength is observed at seven times its emitted wavelength. This is why
        Webb&rsquo;s infrared instruments can recover ultraviolet and optical information from the young
        universe. Redshift is not itself the unusual redness used to select the class.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>An intrinsically unusual colour</h3>
      <p>Little red dots typically have blue rest-frame ultraviolet light but a sharply rising, red
        rest-frame optical continuum: a characteristic V-shaped spectral-energy distribution. Strong
        structure near the hydrogen Balmer limit deepens the puzzle.</p>
      <p class="formula">\[
        \lambda_{\rm Balmer}=364.6\,{\rm nm},
        \qquad
        z=6\Longrightarrow
        \lambda_{\rm obs}\simeq2.55\,\mu{\rm m}.
      \]</p>
      <p>That falls squarely within Webb&rsquo;s near-infrared reach. A colour image supplies the invitation;
        spectroscopy supplies the physical evidence. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap">
  <div class="grid three">
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>Little</h3>
      <p>The bright cores are unresolved or extremely compact, often constrained to scales of roughly a
        hundred parsecs or less. &ldquo;Little&rdquo; is angular and structural, not a statement that the source
        contains little mass or energy.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card illuminated">
      <h3>Red</h3>
      <p>The red optical continuum, Balmer structure and blue ultraviolet side together resist a simple old,
        dusty stellar-population fit. Gas can absorb, scatter and re-emit the central radiation in ways that
        imitate both stars and an active nucleus.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>Dots</h3>
      <p>Surface-brightness dimming can erase a faint surrounding galaxy while leaving its central source
        visible. In 2026, stacked imaging of 217 objects recovered extended rest-frame optical hosts around
        the compact centres. A dot can be the visible tip of a galaxy.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

