<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">The spectral riddle</p>
    <h2 id="t-clues">Five Clues One Model Must Explain Together</h2>
    <p>A successful account cannot select only the convenient evidence. It must explain why the same sources
      can look star-like in one diagnostic, black-hole-like in another, and strangely quiet in the bands where
      an exposed active galactic nucleus should shout.</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Compactness and luminosity:</strong> a very small region emits far too much light to be an
        ordinary star and can be uncomfortably dense if all the light is assigned to stars.</li>
      <li><strong>Broad hydrogen and helium lines:</strong> many spectra contain extended line wings, often
        treated as evidence for fast gas orbiting a massive black hole.</li>
      <li><strong>A Balmer break or turnover:</strong> the continuum can resemble a stellar atmosphere or an
        evolved stellar population even where those explanations create severe density or age problems.</li>
      <li><strong>Weak X-ray and radio emission:</strong> many sources lack the loud coronal and jet signatures
        expected from familiar unobscured active nuclei.</li>
      <li><strong>Little variability:</strong> many do not flicker as strongly as a bare, compact accretion flow
        might, suggesting that direct light is being reprocessed across a larger envelope.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="lede">The dense-cocoon model is compelling because one physical arrangement addresses all five;
      it is not compelling merely because the words &ldquo;black hole&rdquo; sound dramatic.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap">
  <div class="page-head tight">
    <p class="kicker">The leading physical picture</p>
    <h2 id="t-cocoon">A Black Hole Inside a Radiating Envelope</h2>
    <p class="lede muted">A central accretor supplies the power. Dense gas changes the spectrum before the
      radiation escapes.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="grid two">
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>The hidden engine</h3>
      <p>Gas falling toward a black hole converts gravitational binding energy into radiation. Near the
        Eddington scale, outward radiation pressure competes with inward gravity:</p>
      <p class="formula">\[
        L_{\rm Edd}=\frac{4\pi G M_{\rm BH}m_pc}{\sigma_T}
        \simeq1.26\times10^{38}
        \left(\frac{M_{\rm BH}}{M_\odot}\right)
        {\rm erg\,s^{-1}}.
      \]</p>
      <p>A young black hole accreting near or above that scale can be intensely luminous while remaining much
        less massive than an early interpretation based on all of the surrounding light.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card illuminated">
      <h3>The disguising cocoon</h3>
      <p>If the electron column density \(N_e\) is high, the Thomson optical depth approaches or exceeds one:</p>
      <p class="formula">\[
        \tau_e=N_e\sigma_T\gtrsim1.
      \]</p>
      <p>Photons then scatter repeatedly before escape. The gas absorbs high-energy radiation, reprocesses
        ionising light into optical nebular emission, and creates broad symmetric line wings. The emergent
        source can resemble a cool stellar photosphere even though accretion, not fusion, pays the energy bill.
        <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">Why the inferred mass changed</p>
    <h2 id="t-line-width">A Broad Line Need Not Be a Fast Orbit</h2>
    <p>A conventional virial black-hole estimate treats a line width \(\Delta v\) as orbital motion in a
      broad-line region of scale \(R\):</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      M_{\rm vir}\sim f\,\frac{R(\Delta v)^2}{G}.
    \]</p>
    <p>If electron scattering contributes much of the width, then \(\Delta v\) is not a clean orbital speed.
      Substituting it into the virial formula makes the black hole appear too massive. High-quality spectra
      published in January 2026 resolved relatively narrow intrinsic cores beneath scattering wings and inferred
      masses of roughly \(10^5\) to \(10^7M_\odot\) for that sample &mdash; about two orders of magnitude
      below earlier broad-line estimates. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
    <p>This does not make every mass estimate small. Abell2744-QSO1 has a spatially resolved rotation curve
      consistent with a central point mass of about \(5\times10^7M_\odot\), independently demonstrating that
      at least one prototypical little red dot really does contain a supermassive black hole. Different methods
      answer different questions, and the class spans more than one mass scale.</p>
  </div>
</section>

