<div class="divider">✦</div>

<section class="wrap">
  <div class="page-head tight">
    <p class="kicker">What changed in 2026</p>
    <h2 id="t-evidence">Six Pieces That Now Fit Better</h2>
    <p class="lede muted">No single result settles an entire population. Together, these observations and
      calculations have moved the black-hole interpretation from an attractive possibility to the leading
      account for many little red dots.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="grid two">
    <div class="frame card illuminated">
      <h3>Ionised cocoons</h3>
      <p>Deep spectra of a high-quality sample showed line profiles consistent with electron scattering in
        compact gas with \(\tau_e\sim1\). The inferred central masses fell to \(10^5\)&ndash;\(10^7M_\odot\),
        while accretion remained near the Eddington scale. This joins the broad lines, X-ray weakness and
        optical continuum in one model. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>A direct central mass</h3>
      <p>Gravitational lensing and spatially resolved spectroscopy of Abell2744-QSO1 produced a Keplerian
        rotation curve around a point mass of about fifty million Suns, with little room for a comparably
        massive stellar host. It is evidence for a massive seed caught before its galaxy has caught up.
        <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>More than forty lines</h3>
      <p>The lensed source GLIMPSE-17775 yielded the deepest little-red-dot spectrum then available. An
        &ldquo;iron forest&rdquo;, oxygen ratios, helium fluorescence and absorption all point toward a powerful
        accretor inside a dense medium. One object does not define the class, but this one carries many
        independent diagnostics. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card illuminated">
      <h3>A black-hole-star exemplar</h3>
      <p>MoM-BH*-1 combines an extreme Balmer break, broad multi-peaked hydrogen emission and absorption from
        thick gas. A gas-enshrouded, gas-reddened black hole explains the source without assigning its whole
        luminosity to a fantastically dense mature galaxy. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card illuminated">
      <h3>The hosts emerge</h3>
      <p>Stacking 217 Webb targets revealed faint extended rest-frame optical components consistent with
        compact star-forming hosts of roughly \(10^9M_\odot\) and mean radii near 210 parsecs. A central
        black hole and a host galaxy are not rival answers; they can be two scales of the same system.
        <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>A formation route in simulation</h3>
      <p>Radiation-hydrodynamic simulations produced heavy seeds around \(10^6M_\odot\), dense scattering
        discs, broad H\(\alpha\), and super-Eddington growth to about \(3\times10^7M_\odot\) by \(z\simeq8\).
        This demonstrates physical possibility within a model; it does not by itself prove that nature used
        that route for every observed source. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">The remaining plurality</p>
    <h2 id="t-not-one-thing">Why the Case Is Strong but Not Closed</h2>
    <p>Colour selection gathers sources before their engines are known. A category defined by compactness and
      a V-shaped continuum can contain several physical mixtures:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>an accreting black hole plus a dense, mostly dust-free gas envelope;</strong></li>
      <li><strong>an obscured active nucleus plus a compact star-forming host;</strong></li>
      <li><strong>a dense dusty starburst whose stellar light dominates;</strong></li>
      <li><strong>a narrow-line, low-mass accretor or a star-dominated system;</strong></li>
      <li><strong>a short-lived supermassive-star stage proposed as a black-hole precursor.</strong></li>
    </ul>
    <p>The first two now explain much of the best spectroscopy. The others remain live for subsets or as
      competing descriptions of particular objects. The right scientific move is not to preserve mystery for
      its own sake, nor to erase diversity after one success. It is to sort the population with better spectra,
      resolved hosts, variability, polarisation and multi-wavelength limits.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider">✦</div>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">The growth problem</p>
    <h2 id="t-growth">How a Seed Becomes Enormous Before the Universe Grows Old</h2>
    <p>At a fixed Eddington ratio \(\lambda=L/L_{\rm Edd}\) and radiative efficiency \(\epsilon\), idealised
      exponential growth can be written</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      M(t)=M_{\rm seed}\exp\!\left[
      \frac{1-\epsilon}{\epsilon}\,
      \frac{\lambda\,\Delta t}{t_{\rm Edd}}
      \right],
      \qquad t_{\rm Edd}\simeq0.45\ {\rm Gyr}.
    \]</p>
    <p>For \(\epsilon\simeq0.1\) and \(\lambda\simeq1\), one e-fold takes roughly fifty million years.
      Starting earlier, starting heavier, accreting above the nominal Eddington rate, or reducing radiative
      efficiency can each ease the race. Feedback can also stop the supply, so the smooth exponential is an
      upper-level guide rather than a biography of one object.</p>
    <p>Little red dots matter because they may reveal the growth episode itself: the central black hole is
      feeding, the gas is still close enough to hide it, and the host has not yet settled onto the black-hole
      versus stellar-mass relation seen nearby. Their apparent disappearance can then be an evolutionary
      change, not annihilation. The cocoon thins; the host grows; the nucleus becomes an ordinary active galaxy
      or quasar and no longer passes the little-red-dot colour cuts. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap">
  <div class="page-head tight">
    <p class="kicker">Decisive work still to do</p>
    <h2 id="t-tests">What the Next Observations Must Test</h2>
  </div>
  <div class="grid three">
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>Resolve the line wings</h3>
      <p>Do many objects show the symmetric exponential wings expected from electron scattering, or velocity
        structures that demand orbiting or outflowing gas?</p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>Find the hidden hosts</h3>
      <p>Deeper imaging and lensing should separate the point-like nucleus from extended stars and measure
        \(M_{\rm BH}/M_\star\) without assigning one component&rsquo;s light to the other.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>Watch them change</h3>
      <p>Variability and reverberation place size limits on the emitter. A large reprocessing envelope should
        smooth and delay changes from the compact central engine.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card illuminated">
      <h3>Count them consistently</h3>
      <p>A uniform spectroscopic census over \(2&lt;z&lt;10\) can distinguish a true evolutionary peak from
        changing filters, sensitivity, contamination and selection definitions.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>Measure the missing energy</h3>
      <p>Deeper X-ray, radio, submillimetre and mid-infrared data must locate where absorbed power re-emerges.
        Obscuration can hide radiation; it cannot delete energy.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>Test the seed pathway</h3>
      <p>Metallicity, environment, companions and duty cycle can discriminate light stellar-remnant seeds from
        heavy direct-collapse seeds and supermassive-star precursors.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider">✦</div>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame frame--creed">
    <p class="kicker">What this means here</p>
    <h2 id="t-framework">A Test of Discipline for The Shape of Becoming</h2>
    <p>Little red dots are exactly the kind of discovery that can tempt a metaphysical framework into
      retrospective possession: compact, early, red, hidden, transitional, apparently too mature. None of
      those adjectives makes them evidence for a cosmic torus, a Möbius seam, the \(\chi\)-field, the Prime
      Plane, consciousness as integration, or a residue travelling between universe-instances.</p>
    <p>The scientifically earned account uses accretion, radiative transfer, electron scattering, stellar
      populations, cosmological redshift and selection effects. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span> The framework
      may use an enshrouded seed becoming visible as an <em>analogy</em> for hidden integration becoming form.
      <span class="myth">&#9670;</span> Analogy is not prediction.</p>
    <p>If this cosmology wants to claim more, it must calculate something the standard models do not already
      supply: a distinct redshift distribution, line-profile relation, host-mass scaling, orientation signal,
      or time evolution fixed in advance and exposed to failure. Until then, the little red dots belong to
      observational astrophysics, and the honest response of this site is wonder without annexation.</p>
  </div>
</section>

